<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Code of Hammurabi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1754 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century-bce">18th century BCE</a> · Also known as Hammurabi's famous Code</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Code of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a> is a Babylonian law code issued around 1754 BCE by Hammurabi, king of the Babylonian Empire, inscribed on a basalt stele. It contains nearly 300 laws on commerce, property, family and crime, including the principle of proportional punishment. The stele was carried off to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elamites">Elamites</a> and rediscovered there, and it is now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/code-of-hammurabi.jpg" alt="Code of Hammurabi" width="255" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mbzt · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:P1050763_Louvre_code_Hammurabi_face_rwk.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Code is a Babylonian legal text in the Old Babylonian dialect of Akkadian, purportedly by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a>, sixth king of the Amorite First Dynasty of Babylon, who ruled from around 1792 to around 1750 BCE by the middle chronology. When he inherited the throne <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> held little sway, and the local power was Rim-Sin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/larsa">Larsa</a>. Hammurabi conquered his territory in one campaign. His letters suggest an interest in law and justice, and he often calls himself his people's shepherd. The Code was not the first Mesopotamian law collection: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/code-of-ur-nammu">Code of Ur-Nammu</a>, the Code of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lipit-ishtar">Lipit-Ishtar</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laws-of-eshnunna">Laws of Eshnunna</a> are earlier, and they too claim a ruler as author.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The main copy is a basalt stele 2.25 m tall, with a relief at the top of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utu">Shamash</a>, the sun god and god of justice. Below are about 4,130 lines of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a>, one fifth a poetic prologue and epilogue and four fifths the laws. In the prologue Hammurabi says the gods chose him to rule in order to prevent the strong from oppressing the weak. The laws are conditional sentences, if one thing happens then another follows, and cover criminal, family, property and commercial law, including rules on medical treatment. They apply the principle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lex-talionis">lex talionis</a>: law 196 prescribes an eye for an eye, and law 229 requires the death of a builder whose house collapses and kills its owner.</p><p>Nobles had greater protection than commoners and enslaved people. Scholars dispute what the text was. Some treat it as legislation, others as a law report of past cases, and others as an abstract work of jurisprudence, a view that has gained support among Assyriologists.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The stele was found in 1901 at Susa in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, where it had been taken as plunder about 600 years after it was made. It was found in three large fragments and now stands in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a>. Seven columns near the bottom were polished away in antiquity, but over 50 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> of the laws are known, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nineveh">Nineveh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assur">Assur</a> and other sites, and they fill in most of the erased text. Scribes copied the text for over a millennium. Father Jean-Vincent Scheil published the first edition in 1902, and editions in German, English and Italian followed. Scholars compared its provisions with the Mosaic Law, but the consensus is that the similarities come from shared traditions and not direct borrowing.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Code of Hammurabi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Hammurabi's famous Code</b>, inscribed on a basalt stele now in the Louvre, is as much an economic regulatory document as a legal one.</blockquote><a href="/empire/babylonian-empire">Babylonian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Code of Hammurabi</b> was found at Susa, not Babylon: Elamite kings carried it off as a war trophy c. 1158 BCE, and French excavators recovered it around 1900.</blockquote><a href="/empire/elamite-confederation">Elamite Confederation · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Ur-Nammu, who ruled c. 2047–2030 BCE by one chronology and c. 2112–2094 BCE by another, built temples including the Ziggurat of Ur, improved agriculture by irrigation and issued the Code of Ur-Nammu, which precedes the <b>Code of Hammurabi</b> by 300 years.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ur">Ur · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Code of Hammurabi</b> sorts people into three classes, the free man (awīlum), the commoner (muškēnum) and the slave, and punishes by class.</blockquote><a href="/essays/from-ruler-to-enslaved">From Ruler to Enslaved</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Code of Hammurabi</b>, written near the end of his reign, sorts people into three classes and punishes by class: the awīlum, the free man; the muškēnum, a commoner whose exact standing scholars still debate; and the slave, wardum or amtum.</blockquote><a href="/societies/old-babylonia">Social Hierarchy of Old Babylonia</a></li></ul></section>
